page('index.html','Use Q-CRAFT to build and explain a fiscal comparison',
'Q-CRAFT, the Quantitative Climate Risk Assessment Fiscal Tool, connects long-run growth assumptions and climate scenarios to government finances. This companion explains how to run the Explorer and assess what its results support.',f'''
<div class="summary">Choose the data, check the fiscal baseline, compare scenarios, and retain a result you can explain. The output is a conditional projection. Its value depends on the assumptions and the question you ask.</div>
<ol class="model-map" aria-label="How the model fits together"><li><b>Debt dynamics</b><span>Growth, financing costs and the primary balance determine the debt path.</span></li><li><b>Build the baseline</b><span>Population, productivity, prices and fiscal assumptions extend the medium-term data.</span></li><li><b>Add climate effects</b><span>Productivity changes affect GDP, revenue and spending, then debt.</span></li></ol>
<h2 id="run">Complete your first run</h2>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open the Explorer and select your country.</strong> Use Current for the refreshed data or Verified to compare with the published workbook. Read the dates shown beside the run. <a href="data.html">See what differs between the modes.</a></li>
<li><strong>Inspect the baseline.</strong> Compare starting debt and fiscal figures with country sources using the same definition and period. Look for a debt floor, rule response or implausible spending path before interpreting the climate gap. <a href="results.html#baseline">Use the five baseline checks.</a></li>
<li><strong>Review the assumptions.</strong> Open each control’s context and record the reason for your choices, including retained defaults. The <a href="assumptions.html">settings guide</a> explains which fields are active and when they affect results.</li>
<li><strong>Compare one scenario at a stated year.</strong> Read both its GDP effect and its debt-ratio difference. Change spending rigidity while holding the climate scenario fixed, then explain why the debt result changes. This tests a fiscal assumption. It does not estimate an adaptation programme’s return.</li>
<li><strong>Write down the finding and its limits.</strong> Name the country, data mode, settings, scenario, comparison year and scenario start. Explain the transmission through GDP and the primary balance. Flag evidence still to check.</li>
<li><strong>Export and reopen.</strong> The Export tab offers a packet with the run file, data and readable outputs. Save it, then import the run file and check its identity and settings. <a href="results.html#export">Read the export and replay guidance.</a></li></ol>
<p><a class="button" href="{APP}">Open Q-CRAFT Explorer</a></p>
<h2>Choose the reference you need</h2>
<div class="cards"><a href="data.html"><b>IMF workbook and updated data</b><span>Versions, dates and the forecast-to-model transition.</span></a><a href="model.html"><b>Understand the model</b><span>The equations and the fiscal climate mechanism.</span></a><a href="assumptions.html"><b>Choose the settings</b><span>All twelve controls and the judgments they require.</span></a><a href="results.html"><b>Assess and save a result</b><span>Baseline checks, interpretation, export and feedback.</span></a><a href="workbook.html"><b>Run the official Excel workbook</b><span>Downloads and the corresponding worksheet sequence.</span></a></div>
<h2>About this companion</h2><p>This is the operating companion for the refreshed Explorer. The longer workshop course and its worked examples are being revised separately. Teal Insights develops the Explorer as an independent implementation of the IMF model. The IMF workbook and User Guide remain the primary references, with the dated differences explained here.</p>
''')

page('data.html','The workbook comparison and the updated data',
'Verified retains the data window in the published IMF workbook. Current uses the full usable newer WEO window, then starts the long-run assumptions. This changes both the baseline and the period covered by the additional climate comparison.',f'''
<h2 id="versions">The two versions, with Uganda as the example</h2>
<div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Dates refer to the workbook identified below and the April 2026 Current revision.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Input or boundary</th><th scope="col">Verified</th><th scope="col">Current</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Macrofiscal data</th><td>WEO October 2024</td><td>WEO April 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Last supplied WEO year, Uganda</th><td>2029</td><td>2031</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Long-run assumptions, Uganda</th><td>Start in 2030</td><td>Start in 2032</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Additional climate comparison, Uganda</th><td>Start in 2030, anchored at 2029</td><td>Start in 2032, anchored at 2031</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Demography</th><td>UN World Population Prospects 2022</td><td>UN World Population Prospects 2024</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Historical labour productivity</th><td>Workbook’s WDI series, through 2022 for Uganda</td><td>Retained from the workbook</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Climate paths</th><td>Workbook’s 2024 FADCP climate data</td><td>Same data, on its original calendar</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The 2024 and 2026 WEO releases each provide a medium-term window. Current retains all usable years from the newer release instead of cutting it off at the old workbook’s 2029 endpoint. The selected run shows the supported boundary for that country. Missing required inputs can shorten the usable window or prevent a projection.</p>
<p>The WEO years contain source estimates and projections. This extract does not establish one verified outturn-versus-forecast cutoff for all country series. A last WEO year tells you where supplied data ends, not when historical observations ended.</p>
<h2 id="climate-boundary">What the later climate start means</h2>
<p>The IMF Guide separates its long-run climate comparison from the near- and medium-term period. Current carries that structure forward after the newer WEO window. Moving this boundary is an Explorer choice. The dated IMF Guide does not prescribe a 2032 start or validate this extension.</p>
<p>For Uganda Current, all scenario paths follow the supplied baseline through 2031. The first additional climate effect uses the change in the climate index between calendar years 2031 and 2032. The 2030 and 2031 incremental effects are omitted from this comparison. They are not delayed and added back later. The climate dataset’s historical temperature reference is unchanged.</p>
<p>The WEO baseline is a supplied macrofiscal forecast, not a demonstrated climate-free counterfactual. Describe the output as an incremental scenario comparison from its stated start year. Results will differ from the workbook because the macrofiscal and demographic inputs, starting levels and comparison period differ.</p>
<details><summary>Check the annual climate calculation</summary><p>Let J(y) = 100 + the supplied climate GDP-loss percentage in year y. The annual productivity-growth adjustment is 100 × [J(y) / J(y−1) − 1]. For Current Uganda it is applied from 2032. Multiplying the annual index factors through year T gives J(T) / J(2031). This is an identity for the climate driver, not an exact identity for the model’s resulting GDP ratio.</p></details>
{ref(19, 'Climate Data and scenario worksheets')}
<h2 id="productivity">How WEO-implied productivity is calculated</h2>
<p>The IMF workbook derives a labour-productivity growth residual between the end of its World Development Indicators (WDI) history and the end of WEO. It combines real GDP growth with an employment-growth proxy. It is not a separately published WEO productivity forecast or an Explorer invention.</p>
<div class="equation">1 + productivity growth = (1 + real GDP growth) / (1 + employment growth)</div>
<p>Use decimal rates in this identity and multiply the resulting productivity rate by 100 to express it as a percentage. The employment proxy uses working-age population growth. In Uganda Current, WDI levels end in 2022 and the residual covers 2023–2031. Long-run productivity assumptions then take over in 2032. The displayed bridge must be read for the selected country and data mode.</p>
{ref(12, 'Baseline!R12:X12 for the 2023–2029 residual in the published file')}
<h2 id="source-dates">Why the PDF and Excel dates differ</h2>
<p>The User Guide is dated October 2024 and identifies version 1.0_11-04-2024. Its productivity explanation uses WDI through 2021, a residual through 2028 and projections from 2029. The downloadable workbook checked here identifies version 1.0_11-15-2024 on Dashboard!B10. It uses WDI through 2022, WEO through 2029 and long-run projections from 2030. Verified follows that later workbook’s actual inputs and formulas.</p>
<p><a href="{WORKBOOK}">Download the reference IMF workbook</a> · <a href="{GUIDE}">Download the IMF User Guide</a> · <a href="{IMF}">Check the IMF’s current downloads</a></p>
<h2 id="revisions">Keep the data revision with the result</h2><p>April 2026 names a source release. It does not distinguish the older Explorer’s truncated data from this full-window revision. New exports include data and calculation identities, the usable WEO endpoint and the climate start. Importing an older Current file restores its settings with a warning that the updated inputs and calculation policy can change results. Retain its original packet if you need the old numbers.</p>
''')

page('model.html','Follow the calculation from growth to debt',
'The model constructs a fiscal baseline, then changes productivity under climate scenarios. The debt equation connects the real economy and public finances.',f'''
<h2 id="debt">Debt dynamics</h2>
<div class="equation">d(t) = [(1 + r(t)) / (1 + g(t))] × d(t−1) − pb(t)</div>
<p>Here d is debt divided by GDP, r is the nominal interest rate on government debt, g is nominal GDP growth, and pb is the primary surplus divided by GDP. Use decimal rates consistently. A larger primary surplus reduces debt. A higher interest-growth multiplier raises the contribution of inherited debt.</p>
<p>The basic equation omits exchange-rate valuation and stock-flow adjustments. The workbook-shaped baseline also floors debt at zero, while climate scenarios allow negative debt, interpreted as net assets. Once that floor binds, a scenario gap can reflect the asymmetry as well as the climate transmission.</p>
{ref(16,'Baseline!Y36 and Hot!Y35')}
<h2 id="growth">Construct real and nominal GDP</h2>
<div class="equation">Real GDP = labour productivity × employment<br>1 + real GDP growth = (1 + productivity growth) × (1 + employment growth)<br>1 + nominal GDP growth = (1 + real GDP growth) × (1 + GDP-deflator growth)</div>
<p>Labour productivity measures output per employed person. Employment growth after WEO is proxied by working-age population growth, holding the employment-to-working-age-population ratio constant. Faster population growth therefore affects this projection through an assumption about employment. It does not establish that the economy will create those jobs.</p>
<p>Historical productivity levels, the <a href="data.html#productivity">WEO-period residual</a>, and the user’s long-run productivity path play different roles. The productivity controls set an asymmetric convergence path after WEO. Its timing parameter shifts that path. The setting is not a halfway-growth date and the start parameter need not equal the first computed annual value.</p>
{ref(25,'Baseline!Y7:Y15 and Productivity!B24')}
<h2 id="fiscal">Construct the fiscal baseline</h2>
<p>After WEO, nominal revenue grows with nominal GDP. Primary expenditure grows with productivity, prices and total population, with those three factors multiplied together. If the fiscal rule is on, the previous year’s adjustment is then added in currency units. Primary balance equals revenue less primary expenditure, excluding interest.</p>
<p>The distinction between working-age and total population matters: one drives the employment proxy and GDP, while the other enters baseline spending. The debt target activates a fiscal response under the rule’s conditions. It is not a hard cap, a plan for specific spending cuts or evidence that the adjustment is politically feasible. The model has no feedback from that adjustment to GDP.</p>
<p>Financing assumptions determine the weighted average nominal interest rate after WEO. The constant-real and normalized interest-growth-differential approaches use lagged baseline inflation or growth. Climate scenarios retain the baseline interest-rate path.</p>
{ref(29,'Baseline!Y25:Y29, X42 and Interest Rate!B17:B19')}
<h2 id="climate">Trace the climate effect through the budget</h2>
<ol><li>The scenario supplies an adjustment to productivity growth.</li><li>Employment and prices follow the baseline assumptions, so the productivity adjustment changes real and nominal GDP.</li><li>Revenue retains the baseline GDP share. A lower GDP level therefore reduces revenue in currency.</li><li>Expenditure rigidity determines how much primary spending adjusts to the GDP change.</li><li>The resulting primary balance and GDP denominator alter the debt ratio.</li></ol>
<p>Rigidity 1 keeps spending at its baseline currency level in each year. Rigidity 0 keeps its baseline share of GDP. Intermediate settings divide that adjustment proportionately. The debt gap consequently depends on the fiscal response as well as the climate path.</p>
{ref(19,'Hot!Y8:Y14, Y24:Y28 and Y35')}
<h2 id="limits">Match the model to the question</h2><p>The built-in scenarios address persistent temperature-related effects through productivity. They do not simulate a particular drought’s output loss, emergency spending, inflation and recovery. They also omit sea-level rise, tipping points and the costs of adaptation. The IMF workbook separately accepts manual discrete fiscal overlays. That workbook facility is not implemented by this Explorer.</p><p>The reference climate trajectory follows the historical temperature trend. It is not a world without warming. An adaptation-speed comparison changes the empirical adjustment path under Hot temperatures. It does not cost or evaluate a named investment programme.</p>
''')

page('assumptions.html','Choose assumptions you can explain',
'The starting settings are a place to begin analysis. Retaining a default is still a choice. Record the country evidence, a sensitivity rationale or the fact that the choice remains provisional.',f'''
<h2 id="country">Country</h2><p>Choose the economy and check its data mode, usable WEO window and coverage notice. Compare debt using the same institutional coverage, period and valuation as the loaded series. A government’s published debt figure can differ for legitimate definitional reasons. Resolve those differences before interpreting the result.</p>
<h2 id="demography">Demography variant</h2><p>Medium, High and Low select UN population variants. Working-age population drives the employment proxy. Total population enters baseline spending. These are demographic scenarios, not an estimate of employment-policy effectiveness. Current uses UN WPP 2024 and Verified uses the workbook’s WPP 2022.</p>
<h2 id="productivity">Three productivity settings</h2><ul><li>Start: the starting parameter of the post-WEO convergence path.</li><li>Long run: the growth rate that the path approaches.</li><li>Turning point: timing in years after the WEO endpoint. A larger value shifts convergence later. The curve is asymmetric.</li></ul><p>Inspect the historical record and WEO-implied residual, then assess whether the chosen trajectory is plausible. For Uganda Current, a timing setting of 15 is anchored to 2031, giving the calendar reference 2046. It is not a promise that growth reaches a particular halfway value then.</p>
<h2 id="prices">Two inflation settings</h2><p>Start and long run set the post-WEO GDP-deflator growth path. They refer to the price measure used to turn real into nominal GDP, not necessarily the consumer-price inflation target. Supplied WEO values remain through the usable endpoint. The convergence formula determines the first modelled value.</p>
<h2 id="interest">Interest-rate approach and long-run real rate</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Approach</th><th scope="col">What happens after WEO</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Nominal interest rate</th><td>Holds the rate at the final usable WEO anchor.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Interest-growth differential</th><td>Retains the normalized differential, (r−g)/(1+g), using lagged baseline growth in the projection rule.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Real interest rate</th><td>Combines the chosen real rate with prior-year inflation using the exact compound relationship.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The long-run real-rate field is active only under the Real interest rate approach. Recording a value in an inactive field does not mean it affected the result. These approaches do not model a sovereign yield curve or changing debt portfolio.</p>
<h2 id="fiscal-rule">Debt target and fiscal rule</h2><p>With the rule on, the target enters the next-year spending adjustment when the workbook’s debt-level and direction conditions apply. With the rule off, the target does not constrain the debt path. Check the resulting spending and balance paths, including any debt floor. The Guide suggests examining the baseline without the rule, while the downloaded workbook contains saved settings with the rule on. Neither removes the need to state your own purpose.</p>
<h2 id="rigidity">Expenditure rigidity</h2><p>This setting applies to climate scenarios. At 1, each year’s primary spending stays at the baseline currency amount despite the GDP change. At 0, it stays at the baseline share of GDP. A lower setting can reduce the debt gap by allowing spending to fall with GDP. Whether such adjustment is feasible or desirable needs country evidence.</p><p>A comparison using 1 and 0.6 can reveal this mechanism. The value 0.6 is an illustrative sensitivity, not an estimate for Uganda. Hold other settings and the climate scenario fixed, record the difference, then restore the intended headline assumption before exporting.</p>
<h2>Record all twelve settings</h2><p>The twelve fields are country, demography, three productivity settings, two inflation settings, interest-rate approach, long-run real rate, debt target, fiscal rule and expenditure rigidity. The data mode and calculation revision also belong with the run. Distinguish evidence-backed assumptions, retained starting values and sensitivity choices in the rationale notes.</p>
{ref(10,'Dashboard and the Productivity, Inflation and Interest Rate worksheets')}
''')

page('results.html','Check, explain and retain the comparison',
'A useful output names the comparison and explains why it changes. Check the baseline first, then interpret the climate result with the chosen settings and model limits alongside it.',f'''
<h2 id="baseline">Five baseline checks</h2><ul><li>Have you compared the loaded debt figures with the same period, coverage and valuation in a country source?</li><li>Is the revenue-to-GDP ratio plausible given WEO forecasts?</li><li>Does the expenditure path reflect realistic spending trends?</li><li>Can you explain the fiscal-rule response, any target crossings and whether the baseline debt floor binds?</li><li>Do the primary and overall balance paths make economic sense given the country’s fiscal history?</li></ul>
<h2 id="analysis">Read a scenario difference</h2><p>Choose the year and identify both paths. A debt-ratio difference is measured in percentage points of GDP. A GDP difference expressed relative to the baseline is a percentage. Check the units on the chart and in the data table. Record whether a path has crossed zero or hit a floor before describing the gap.</p><p>The displayed range across the six built-in scenarios is a range of those model runs. It is not a probability interval or an exhaustive range of future climate outcomes. State the mechanism: changed productivity affects GDP, revenue and the spending response, then debt. The size of the debt gap depends on baseline fiscal assumptions.</p>
<h2 id="climate">Read the climate and adaptation scenarios</h2><p>Paris, Moderate, High and Hot use different global temperature assumptions. Hot adapted and Hot unadapted hold the Hot temperature path while changing the assumed speed of economic adjustment. They do not specify the spending, financing or design of a Ugandan adaptation programme. A national mitigation commitment also does not select the global Paris path.</p><p>A no-estimate notice means the supplied climate slice cannot establish the country’s climate effect. Overlapping paths in that case are not evidence that the country is safe from climate risk.</p>
<h2 id="data">Use the data table to check the chart</h2><p>Read the same country, mode, scenario and year in the data view. Reconcile a suspicious chart value before writing about it. The WEO-to-model boundary and the scenario start should agree with the run’s metadata. If a parameter change appears not to matter, check whether it is inactive or whether you are looking at supplied WEO years.</p>
<h2 id="export">Save a result and reopen it</h2><ol><li>Give the run a recognisable label. Add an analyst’s note stating the finding, purpose and unfinished evidence checks.</li><li>Review the settings and rationale in Export. Restore the headline settings after sensitivities.</li><li>Download the packet, or the individual run file and outputs you need. Keep the data and calculation identities with any chart sent to a colleague.</li><li>Import the saved run file. Check the country, mode, settings and any compatibility warning before treating the new display as a reproduction.</li></ol><p>Exact replay requires compatible calculations and matching inputs. Older Current files predate the full-window revision. Their settings can be reused, but an import warning means their old results have not been reproduced. Keep the original packet for that record.</p>
<h2 id="writing">Write the finding in two paragraphs</h2><p>In the first paragraph, name the data revision, assumptions, scenario, start and comparison year. Give the GDP and debt differences with the right units and explain their fiscal mechanism. In the second, identify the assumptions that need evidence, the sensitivity result and what the model leaves unanswered. Answer three challenges: Is this a forecast? Why this rigidity value? Is the debt gap the cost of climate change?</p><p>The workshop can end with a provisional comparison and clearly marked open work. A completed country analysis needs the reproducible packet, two explanatory paragraphs and two sensitivity comparisons, reviewed for assumptions, baseline, interpretation and writing. This is a completion checklist, not certification for publication.</p>
<h2 id="codesign">Help shape the next iteration</h2><p>This Explorer is an early iteration intended to support ministry analysts’ actual work. After trying a run, note what was hard to find, what result was hard to explain and what output would help you brief senior management. A concrete example of the task and where it stalled is more useful than a general rating. Share that feedback in the workshop or through the Explorer’s feedback link.</p>
''')

page('workbook.html','Run the official IMF workbook alongside the Explorer',
'The IMF publishes an Excel workbook and a User Guide. Keep the workbook as the reference when checking Verified mode, and use the data comparison to understand why Current results differ.',f'''
<div class="downloads"><a class="button" href="{WORKBOOK}" download>Download IMF workbook (.xlsx)</a><a class="button secondary" href="{GUIDE}">Read IMF User Guide (PDF)</a></div><p class="source">These are unchanged reference copies: workbook Dashboard version 1.0_11-15-2024 and October 2024 User Guide version 1.0_11-04-2024. <a href="{IMF}">The IMF page provides the publisher’s downloads.</a></p>
<h2 id="excel">Follow the worksheets</h2><ol><li>Save a working copy of the workbook and open it in Excel. Read the Dashboard instructions and select the country. Keep a separate untouched reference copy for comparisons.</li><li>Inspect Macrofiscal and Demography. Check the loaded figures, period, units and population assumptions.</li><li>Set and inspect Productivity, Inflation and Interest Rate. Use the User Guide alongside the worksheet explanations.</li><li>Read Baseline before the climate results. Check revenue, primary expenditure, balances and debt.</li><li>Inspect the climate scenario worksheets and Output Scenarios. Compare the same scenario, year and assumptions used in the Explorer.</li><li>Save the workbook with a name identifying the country and assumptions, and retain any output you plan to cite.</li></ol>
{ref(8,'Dashboard through Output Baseline and Output Scenarios')}
<h2>Compare like with like</h2><p>Choose Verified in the Explorer and match the workbook’s settings before comparing values. Changing a workbook input invalidates a comparison against the unchanged bundled inputs. Existing validation establishes a tested scope: Teal Insights checked baseline parity for 147 countries, with climate parity claims restricted to ratio metrics. It does not mean every workbook feature or possible custom input is implemented.</p><p>Current offers refreshed WEO and UN inputs and the later transition described in <a href="data.html">the data comparison</a>. The differences are part of what is being compared. Choose the version that suits the task and record it with the result.</p>
<h2>Know which surface supports the task</h2><p>The workbook permits direct data replacement and manual discrete fiscal risks. The Explorer provides controls, linked context, charts and exported run packets around the implemented scenarios. It does not expose every editable worksheet. Use the workbook when those additional facilities are needed, and document the changes.</p>
<h2 id="sources">Primary sources and reproduction details</h2><ul><li><a href="{IMF}">IMF Q-CRAFT page</a>: official tool and User Guide.</li><li><a href="{GUIDE}">IMF User Guide</a>: printed page references in this companion refer to this PDF. Its physical PDF page is one higher.</li><li><a href="{WORKBOOK}">Reference workbook</a>: exact sheet and cell references appear beside the explanations.</li><li><a href="https://www.imf.org/en/publications/sprolls/world-economic-outlook-databases">IMF WEO database access</a>: the IMF directs recent releases to its Data portal. Current uses the April 2026 release.</li><li><a href="https://population.un.org/wpp/">UN World Population Prospects</a>: demographic source.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/Teal-Insights/QCraft-App">Explorer source repository</a>: implementation and validation documentation.</li></ul><p>The Explorer’s exported run records the selected input revision and calculation policy. These identities supplement the source release date when results must be reproduced.</p>
''')

nav=''.join(f'<a href="{file}">{escape(title.split(" to ")[0] if file=="index.html" else {"data.html":"Workbook and updated data","model.html":"Understand the model","assumptions.html":"Choose the settings","results.html":"Check and save results","workbook.html":"Official Excel workbook"}[file])}</a>' for file,title,_,_ in pages)
css='''*{box-sizing:border-box}html{scroll-padding-top:1.5rem}body{margin:0;background:#fafaf7;color:#263a46;font:17px/1.65 -apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",Arial,sans-serif}a{color:#096780;text-underline-offset:.18em}a:hover{color:#143e5a}a:focus-visible,summary:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #0094bc;outline-offset:4px}header{background:#143e5a;color:white;padding:1.15rem max(1.2rem,calc((100vw - 1180px)/2))}header a{color:white;text-decoration:none}.brand{font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.01em}header .sub{font-size:.87rem;opacity:.85;margin-left:1rem}.layout{display:grid;grid-template-columns:245px minmax(0,790px);gap:3rem;max-width:1180px;margin:auto;padding:2.7rem 1.4rem 4rem}nav{font-size:.94rem;align-self:start;position:sticky;top:1.5rem}nav a{display:block;padding:.65rem .75rem;border-bottom:1px solid #d4d0ca;text-decoration:none}nav a[aria-current]{background:#e6f4f8;color:#143e5a;font-weight:700;border-left:3px solid #096780}main{min-width:0}h1,h2,h3{color:#143e5a;line-height:1.22}h1{font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:2.65rem;letter-spacing:-.025em;margin:0 0 1rem;max-width:22ch}h2{font-size:1.45rem;margin:2.7rem 0 .85rem}h3{font-size:1.15rem}.lede{font-size:1.15rem;line-height:1.55;margin-bottom:1.8rem}p{margin:.8rem 0 1.05rem}li{padding-left:.15rem;margin:.65rem 0}.summary{background:#e6f4f8;border-left:4px solid #096780;padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:1.5rem 0}.model-map{list-style:none;margin:1.5rem 0;padding:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:.8rem}.model-map li{background:white;border:1px solid #d4d0ca;padding:1rem;margin:0;font-size:.92rem}.model-map b,.model-map span{display:block}.model-map b{margin-bottom:.4rem;color:#143e5a}.steps li{margin:1.15rem 0}.cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:.85rem}.cards a{display:block;border:1px solid #d4d0ca;background:white;padding:1.1rem;text-decoration:none}.cards b,.cards span{display:block}.cards span{font-size:.94rem;color:#465962;margin-top:.4rem}.button{display:inline-block;background:#143e5a;color:#fff;border:2px solid #143e5a;padding:.65rem 1rem;border-radius:5px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:650;margin:.35rem .5rem .35rem 0}.button:hover{color:white;background:#096780}.secondary{background:white;color:#143e5a}.source,footer{font-size:.86rem;color:#50636e}.table-scroll{overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.95rem;text-align:left}th,td{padding:.75rem;border-bottom:1px solid #d4d0ca;vertical-align:top}thead{background:#143e5a;color:white}tbody th{font-weight:600}caption{text-align:left;margin-bottom:.7rem;color:#50636e}.equation{font-size:1.05rem;background:#e6f4f8;padding:1rem 1.15rem;overflow-wrap:anywhere;line-height:1.8}details{border:1px solid #d4d0ca;padding:.8rem 1rem;margin:1rem 0}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:650;color:#143e5a}footer{margin-top:3rem;border-top:1px solid #d4d0ca;padding-top:1rem}.skip{position:absolute;left:-10000px}.skip:focus{left:1rem;top:1rem;background:white;padding:1rem;z-index:5}@media(max-width:850px){.layout{display:block;padding:1.2rem}nav{position:static;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:0;margin:0 0 2rem;font-size:.87rem}h1{font-size:2.05rem}.model-map{grid-template-columns:1fr}.cards{grid-template-columns:1fr}header .sub{display:block;margin:0;font-size:.8rem}body{font-size:16px}th,td{padding:.6rem .45rem;font-size:.88rem}}@media print{header,nav,.skip,.downloads,.button{display:none}.layout{display:block;padding:0;max-width:none}body{background:white;font-size:11pt}h1{font-size:24pt}h2{break-after:avoid}table,.equation,.model-map{break-inside:avoid}a{color:inherit}footer{font-size:9pt}}'''
(OUT/'guide.css').write_text(css)
for file,title,lede,body in pages:
    active=nav.replace(f'href="{file}"',f'href="{file}" aria-current="page"')
    content=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>{escape(title)} | Q-CRAFT Explorer companion</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="guide.css"></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header><a class="brand" href="index.html">Q-CRAFT Explorer</a><span class="sub">Companion · September 2026 revision</span></header><div class="layout"><nav aria-label="Companion chapters">{active}<a href="{APP}">Open Explorer ↗</a></nav><main id="main"><h1>{escape(title)}</h1><p class="lede">{lede}</p>{body}<footer>Teal Insights · Independent implementation of the IMF’s Q-CRAFT. <a href="{IMF}">Official IMF materials</a> · <a href="index.html">Companion contents</a></footer></main></div></body></html>'''
    (OUT/file).write_text(content)
resources=OUT/'resources';resources.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
for name in ['imf-qcraft-tool-v10.xlsx','imf-qcraft-user-guide-v10.pdf']:
    shutil.copy2(HERE/'resources'/name,resources/name)
print(f'Built {len(pages)} companion pages in {OUT}')
